Harbor: Windows.Data.Json works: values, objects and arrays that can be parsed, read, built and written (#95).

Office's web add-in host builds and reads its messages with these classes. CrossOver 26.3's windows.web had
JsonValue.Parse with typed getters and an object that only stored members: no JsonArray class at all, no
Stringify, and no parsing through JsonObject. The add-in host asks for JsonArray, gets "class not registered",
and calls through the missing object: a null-pointer crash on its worker thread, after which the task pane
shows "Loading..." for good.

One node type now backs all three classes: IJsonValue on every node, IJsonObject and IMap<HSTRING, IJsonValue>
on objects, IJsonArray and IVector<IJsonValue> on arrays, with a parser, Stringify, and the statics (Parse,
TryParse, the Create*Value functions; IJsonObjectStatics, IJsonArrayStatics and IJsonValueStatics2 are declared
here because the headers only name them). Objects keep their members in insertion order. Error codes follow
upstream Wine: WEB_E_JSON_VALUE_NOT_FOUND for a missing member, E_ILLEGAL_METHOD_CALL for a value of another
type, WEB_E_INVALID_JSON_STRING for text that doesn't parse.

Not there: GetView on objects and arrays, iteration (IIterable), IJsonObjectWithDefaultValues, IStringable.
Requests for an interface that isn't implemented are logged (fixme:web).

Upstream Wine has since added arrays and an object map, but Stringify and the parse statics are still stubs
there. JsonArray is registered through classes.idl: an existing bottle learns of it when its prefix is updated.

Test: WindowsTests/winrt.

